"""
catpanel: tools for a categorical target in panel data
======================================================

Four steps that run top to bottom in a notebook. All steps share one state dict.

    from catpanel import (step_load, step_explore, step_select,
                          step_diagnostics, step_model)

    state = step_load(".")      # 1: browse, pick files, load
    step_explore(state)         # 1b: look at one column, sum by groups
    step_select(state)          # 2: pick Y and X, filter rows on X values
    step_diagnostics(state)     # 3: distribution checks per group
    step_model(state)           # 4: multinomial logit, checks, imputation, bootstrap

Step 1 can also take a data frame directly: step_load(df=my_df).

Paths in step 1:
    "gs://bucket/folder"   Google Cloud Storage, read with gcsfs
    "gs://"                list buckets in GCS_PROJECT
    anything else          local folder

On your own PC, log in once with:
    gcloud auth application-default login

What is in state after use:
    state["raw"]          data after loading and combining, unfiltered
    state["filtered"]     data after filtering in step 2
    state["selection"]    y, x columns and active filters from step 2
    state["diagnostics"]  summary table from step 3
    state["aggregate"]    last sum table from step 1b
    state["result"]       dict from step 4: data, X, model, probs, calibration,
                          calibration_holdout, summary, bootstrap, flagged,
                          imputation, predictions, settings
    state["predicted"]    filtered data with predicted Y per row

Filtering in step 2: only the chosen values (or range) in an X column decide
which rows are kept. Rows with an empty value in that column are kept.
Y is never filtered. No recoding happens in step 2. Dummy coding and
log transforms happen in step 4.

Step 4 without widgets:
    from catpanel import fit_multinomial, attach_predictions, DEFAULT_SETTINGS
    s = dict(DEFAULT_SETTINGS, y_col="Salary_Group", x_numeric=["Age"],
             x_categorical=["Gender"], id_col="City", date_col="Date",
             year_col="Year", target_year=2025, train_from_year=2015,
             train_to_year=2024)
    result = fit_multinomial(df, s)
    df_out = attach_predictions(df, result)
"""

